---
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import Chart from '../../components/Chart.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import LearnPage from '../../components/learn/LearnPage.astro';
import { source } from '../../data/site.mjs';
import { Pilot, type PilotModel } from '../../engines/pilot';
import lesions from '../../../public/pilot/lesions.json';
import model from '../../../public/pilot/pilot.json';
import pilotCode from '../../../snippets/pilot.py?raw';

const pilotModel = model as unknown as PilotModel;
const evaluation = model.evaluation as Record<string, number>;
const meta = model.meta as { trained: Record<string, number>; history: { step: number; distance: number; rate: number; seconds: number }[] };
const trained = meta.trained;
const minutes = Math.round(meta.history.at(-1)!.seconds / 60);
const percent = (x: number) => `${Math.round(x * 1000) / 10}%`;
const weights = pilotModel.layers.reduce((n, l) => n + (l.kind === 'dense' ? l.inputs * l.outputs + l.outputs : 0), 0);

// The check site/test/pilot.test.ts makes, made again here so the page states what the build saw.
const recorded = JSON.parse(readFileSync(new URL('../../../test/fixtures/pilot.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8'));
let spikes = 0;
let mismatched = 0;
let state = 0;
let steps = 0;
for (const flight of recorded.flights) {
	const pilot = new Pilot(pilotModel);
	pilot.state.set(flight.start);
	for (let t = 0; t < flight.targets.length; t++) {
		for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) pilot.state[i] += flight.kicks[t][i];
		pilot.step(flight.targets[t][0], flight.targets[t][1], flight.box[0], flight.box[1]);
		const fired = [...pilot.spikes.keys()].filter((i) => pilot.spikes[i]);
		spikes += fired.length;
		if (fired.join() !== flight.spikes[t].join()) mismatched++;
		for (let i = 0; i < 6; i++) state = Math.max(state, Math.abs(pilot.state[i] - flight.states[t][i]));
		steps++;
	}
}
if (mismatched) throw new Error(`the browser's pilot fired differently from sparx's on ${mismatched} steps`);
const lesionPoints = lesions.rows.flatMap((r) => r.arrived.map((a) => [r.silenced, a * 100] as [number, number]));
const lesionMeans = lesions.rows.map((r) => [r.silenced, (r.arrived.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / r.arrived.length) * 100] as [number, number]);
---

<LearnPage
	slug="pilot"
	title="The pilot"
	description="How the network on the front page learned to fly a drone with nothing to imitate, how much of it it needs, and how the browser runs the network sparx trained."
>
	<h2>The drone</h2>
	<p>
		The drone is a bar with a rotor at each end, in a vertical plane: mass 1 kg, arms of 25 cm, at most 12 N per rotor, with some air drag. Its
		state is where it is, how fast it moves, its angle and its spin. Every 10 ms the physics takes one semi-implicit Euler step: the two
		thrusts push along the drone's up axis, their difference turns it, and gravity pulls it down. A drone that hits the edge of its box
		bounces off at 30% of its speed.
	</p>
	<p>
		The pilot reads seven numbers each step: the way to its target (clipped to 1.5 m, so a far target reads as "that way"), its velocity, the
		sine and cosine of its angle, and its spin. They enter a dense layer as currents. Two layers of 64 LIF neurons follow, then two leaky
		integrators whose membranes set the rotors' thrust through a sigmoid. Hovering is a membrane of zero. Nothing else carries memory: the
		membranes are the network's whole state.
	</p>
	<CodeBlock code={pilotCode} source={source('site/lab/pilot.py')} />

	<h2>Learning by gradients through physics</h2>
	<p>
		The physics is written in JAX, so a flight is differentiable end to end: through the drone, and through the spikes by their surrogate.
		Each training step flies {trained.batch} drones for {trained.horizon / 100} s from random states, 40% of them at any angle, in boxes from a
		phone's shape to an ultrawide's, chasing targets that jump every 1.2 s on average, through gusts. The loss is their mean distance to their
		targets, plus small penalties on tilt and spin, and on neurons firing outside a band of rates. Adam follows its gradient.
	</p>
	<div class="wide">
		<Chart
			title="Mean distance to the target during training"
			series={[{ points: meta.history.map((h) => [h.step, h.distance] as [number, number]), role: 'learn' }]}
			x={{ label: 'training step', ticks: [0, 1000, 2000, 3000, 4000], min: 0, max: trained.steps }}
			y={{ label: 'distance (m)', ticks: [0, 1, 2], min: 0, max: 2.5, format: (v) => v.toFixed(0) }}
		>
			Mean distance to the target over training flights, which keep moving the target and pushing the drone, so it never reaches zero.
			{trained.steps.toLocaleString('en-US')} steps took {minutes} minutes on a 4-vCPU container.
		</Chart>
	</div>
	<p>
		There is no expert to copy and no reward to estimate: just the derivative of the distance with respect to every weight, through two
		seconds of flight. The gradient is clipped to a norm of 1, which keeps an occasional exploding one from undoing the rest.
	</p>

	<h2>How well it flies</h2>
	<p>
		On 1,000 flights of 6 s to a fixed target from random starts, half of them at any angle, it arrived within 15 cm and stayed there on{' '}
		{percent(evaluation.arrived)}, in a median of {evaluation.median_time_s.toFixed(2)} s. That includes all {evaluation.inverted_starts} starts more
		than a quarter turn from upright. On 1,000 more, thrown and spun at random, with speeds of 4 m/s and spins of 15 rad/s as standard deviations, it arrived on{' '}
		{percent(evaluation.arrived_thrown)}. After 6 s the median drone was {(evaluation.final_distance_median_m * 100).toFixed(1)} cm from its target.
	</p>

	<h2>Silencing neurons</h2>
	<p>
		On the front page you can silence neurons and watch the flying get worse. Here is the same experiment in sparx: silence neurons at random
		from both layers, five times for each count, and fly 500 flights each time.
	</p>
	<div class="wide">
		<Chart
			title="Share of flights that reach the target, against the number of silenced neurons"
			series={[{ points: lesionPoints, role: 'spike', dots: true }, { points: lesionMeans, role: 'ink' }]}
			x={{ label: 'neurons silenced, of 128', ticks: [0, 16, 32, 48, 64, 80, 96, 112], min: 0, max: 112 }}
			y={{ label: 'flights arriving (%)', ticks: [0, 50, 100], min: 0, max: 100 }}
		>
			Each dot is one random set of silenced neurons; the line is their mean.
		</Chart>
	</div>
	<p>
		The network is not uniformly redundant. Most sets of 8 or 16 silenced neurons leave it flying, but one set of 8 grounded it entirely: some
		neurons carry something the rest cannot replace. By 48 the drone mostly falls, and past 64 it never arrives.
	</p>

	<h2>The browser runs sparx's network</h2>
	<p>
		The front page loads the trained weights, {weights.toLocaleString('en-US')} numbers, and steps the network and the physics in TypeScript, in double precision, with
		the same operations in the same order as sparx. When this page was built, it flew sparx's two recorded float64 flights,{' '}
		{steps.toLocaleString('en-US')} steps with targets, gusts and spins, and fired the same {spikes.toLocaleString('en-US')} spikes, every one
		at the same step. The drone's state stayed within {state.toExponential(0)} of sparx's.
	</p>
	<p>
		sparx exports the same network through NIR, the format neuromorphic hardware and other simulators read: <a href="/learn/nir/">NIR export</a>.
	</p>
</LearnPage>
